    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to energy efficiency and clean energy initiatives of the power authority of the state of New York, and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "A9040",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A9040-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1005, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A9040                REVISED 6/22/09\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public authorities law, in relation\nto energy efficiency and clean energy initiatives of the power authority\nof the state of New York, and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\nPurpose:  The purpose of this bill is to enhance the energy efficiency,\nclean energy, energy service and green building programs administered by\nthe Power Authority of the State of New York (NYPA), to reduce energy\nconsumption and mitigate environmental impacts from energy usage\nconsistent with the State's energy and environmental policies.\n\nSummary of provisions:  Section 1 of the bill would add a new subdivi-\nsion 16 to PAL § 1005.  Paragraph (a) of new subdivision 16 would\nprovide and clarify that:  (1) as deemed feasible and advisable by\nNYPA's trustees, NYPA may finance, design, develop, construct, implement\nand administer energy-related projects, programs and services for any\npublic entity and customers of NYPA's low-cost power programs; (2) NYPA\nmay consult standards and other criteria established or adopted by other\norganizations, including but not limited to the United States Green\nBuilding Council under its Leadership in Energy and Environmental Design\n(LEED) programs, the Green Building Initiative's Green Globes Rating\nSystem, and the American National Standards Institute; and (3) NYPA may\nuse note and bond proceeds and other authority funds to finance projects\nand programs.\n\nParagraph (b) of new subdivision 16 would define terms applicable to\nthis subdivision.\n\nParagraph (c) of new subdivision 16 would authorize public entities\n(which include any agency, public authority, public benefit corporation,\npublic corporation, municipal corporation, school district, board of\ncooperative educational services, SUNY or CUNY four year college, or\ncommunity college) to enter into energy services contracts with NYPA for\nenergy-related projects, programs and services, provided that NYPA\nissues and advertises written requests for proposals from third-party\nproviders of goods and services in accordance with its established\nprocurement policies, procedures and guidelines. In addition, this\nprovision provides that NYPA may not contract with a third-party provid-\ner if such provider is listed on a debarment list maintained and\npublished in accordance with New York law.\n\nParagraph (d) of new subdivision 16 would: (1) authorize contracts for\nenergy efficiency, clean energy, energy services and green building\nprograms for up to thirty-five years duration, provided that the dura-\ntion of any such contract does not exceed the expected useful life of\nthe facilities or equipment comprising such projects; (2) require\ncontracts to contain a standard provision that such contracts are deemed\nexecutory only to the extent of the monies appropriated and available\nfor the purpose of the contract; and (3) authorize public entities to\ngrant NYPA access to property in order to allow the construction,\n\ninstallation and operation of facilities and equipment provided as part\nof the energy projects, programs and services.\n\nIn the case of a school district or board of cooperative educational\nservices, paragraph (d) of new subdivision 16 would provide that the\nregulations promulgated by the Commissioner of Education would continue\nto apply, except that a school district or board of cooperative educa-\ntional services may only enter into an energy services contract with\nNYPA for such maximum term as is prescribed in the regulations promul-\ngated by the Commissioner of Education, or for the useful life of the\nfacilities or equipment being constructed, installed or operated, which-\never is less.\n\nParagraph (f) of new subdivision 16 would require NYPA to submit a\nreport on the activities of the program to the Governor, Speaker of the\nAssembly, Temporary President of the Senate, Minority Leader of the\nSenate, Minority Leader of the Assembly, the Chair of the Senate Finance\nCommittee, the Chair of the Assembly Ways and Means Committee, the Chair\nof the Assembly Energy Committee and the Chair of the Senate Energy\nCommittee on or before January 31, 2012.\n\nSection 2 of this bill would provide for an immediate effective date and\nfor the expiration three years after it becomes law.\n\nExisting law:  NYPA is currently authorized by various State enactments\nrelating to Petroleum Overcharge Restitution funds, the Clean\nWater/Clean Air Bond Act of 1996 and other laws to undertake energy\nefficiency and clean energy projects. PAL § 1005 was amended by Chapter\n287 of the Laws of 2004 to authorize NYPA to make energy efficiency\nservices and clean energy technologies available to public and nonpublic\nelementary and secondary schools throughout the State. Chapter 386 of\nthe Laws of 2004 added a new subdivision 15 to PAL § 1005 authorizing\nNYPA to provide similar services to certain military installations in\nNew York.\n\nLegislative history:  This is a new bill.\n\nStatement in support:  This bill would build upon the significant\nreductions in the consumption of energy and the contributions to a\ncleaner environment made possible by NYPA's energy efficiency, clean\nenergy, energy service and sustainable building programs, and make it\neasier for public entities to implement such services and projects.\n\nNYPA has invested more than $1 billion in energy-efficiency projects at\npublicly owned facilities in New York State since 1987, through direct\nimplementation of projects and low-cost financing. Its energy services\nprograms have resulted in more than 1,560 completed projects at 2,640\nfacilities, producing more than $102 million in annual taxpayer savings.\nSince their inception, these programs have reduced energy consumption in\nthe State by over 900 gigawatt hours (GWh) and prevented the emission of\napproximately 824,987 tons of greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nThe expansion of NYPA's energy efficiency programs will also support the\nState's key energy conservation initiatives, including the State's \"45 x\n15\" plan to meet 45 percent of its electricity needs through improved\nenergy efficiency and renewable sources by the year 2015. NYPA's work\nwith State agencies, departments and authorities also supports the goals\nof Executive Order No. 111, which requires agencies to reduce energy\nconsumption, while shifting to on-site use of clean energy technologies.\n\nThis bill would also provide an efficient process for agencies, munici-\npalities, public authorities, public universities, public entities, and\ncustomers of NYPA's low-cost power programs to avail themselves of\nNYPA's expertise and financing, and authorize such entities to enter\ninto contractual arrangements directly with NYPA, provided that NYPA\nissues and advertises a written request for proposals from third party\nproviders of goods and services in accordance with NYPA's own procure-\nment policies, procedures or guidelines.\n\nThis bill would also assist local governments in implementing energy\nconservation programs funded through the American Recovery and Reinvest-\nment Act of 2009.\n\nThis bill would also clarify and confirm the authorization for NYPA's\nsustainable or high performance building program and activities.\n\"Sustainable\" refers to programs that are environmentally responsible\nand resource-efficient throughout a building's life-cycle. Sustainable\nbuilding practices can substantially reduce environmental impacts,\nreduce operating costs, enhance building marketability, and improve\nindoor air quality for building occupants.  Much of NYPA's work in the\nsustainable building area is associated with its energy efficiency/clean\nenergy work. For example, NYPA replaced its chilled water plant at its\noffices in White Plains an energy efficiency measure, and implemented\nother measures that earned the facility LEED (Leadership in Energy and\nEnvironmental Design) Existing Building Gold status. The completed\nproject is saving NYPA 4,704,764 kilowatt hours (kWh) per year of elec-\ntricity, amounting to a savings of $338,951 annually. The new equipment\nhas reduced greenhouse gases by 3,034 tons per year, and mitigates the\nCFCs that would otherwise be in the refrigerants of the old chilled\nwater plant.\n\nMany of the public entities served by NYPA, such as the City of New\nYork, Office of General Services, and State University of New York, wish\nto incorporate sustainable building program measures into the energy\nservice work that NYPA provides. This legislation would make it clear\nthat NYPA may provide all aspects of sustainable development services\nalong with the other measures and services NYPA offers to program\nparticipants.\n\nBudget implications This bill would result in savings to the General\nFund and to public entities because implementation of energy efficiency\nand green building measures will reduce building operating expenses.\n\nEffective date:  This bill takes effect immediately and will expire\nthree years after it becomes law.\n\n",
